To give you the exact code, it helps to know what your website is built with (e.g., standard HTML/CSS, WordPress, React, Shopify) and which email service you use (e.g., Mailchimp, Substack, ConvertKit). 

Here is a clean, modern, and responsive **HTML & CSS** sign-up form you can drop into any website right away:

---

### 1. HTML Code
Paste this where you want the form to appear on your page:

```html
<div class="newsletter-card">
  <div class="newsletter-content">
    <h2>Subscribe to our newsletter</h2>
    <p>Get the latest updates, articles, and resources sent straight to your inbox weekly.</p>
  </div>

  <form class="newsletter-form" action="/your-handling-script" method="POST">
    <div class="input-group">
      <input 
        type="email" 
        name="email" 
        placeholder="Enter your email address" 
        required 
        aria-label="Email address"
      />
      <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
    </div>
    <span class="privacy-text">We respect your privacy. Unsubscribe at any time.</span>
  </form>
</div>
```

---

### 2. CSS Styling
Add this to your stylesheet (or inside `<style>` tags in your `<head>`):

```css
.newsletter-card {
  max-width: 500px;
  margin: 40px auto;
  padding: 32px;
  background-color: #ffffff;
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08);
  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif;
  text-align: center;
}

.newsletter-content h2 {
  margin: 0 0 8px 0;
  color: #1a1a1a;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
}

.newsletter-content p {
  margin: 0 0 24px 0;
  color: #666;
  font-size: 0.95rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
}

.newsletter-form .input-group {
  display: flex;
  gap: 8px;
  flex-direction: column;
}

.newsletter-form input[type="email"] {
  padding: 12px 16px;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
  border-radius: 6px;
  font-size: 1rem;
  outline: none;
  transition: border-color 0.2s;
}

.newsletter-form input[type="email"]:focus {
  border-color: #0066cc;
}

.newsletter-form button {
  padding: 12px 24px;
  background-color: #0066cc;
  color: white;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 6px;
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background-color 0.2s;
}

.newsletter-form button:hover {
  background-color: #0052a3;
}

.privacy-text {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 12px;
  font-size: 0.8rem;
  color: #888;
}

/* Switches to a single row on desktop screens */
@media (min-width: 480px) {
  .newsletter-form .input-group {
    flex-direction: row;
  }

  .newsletter-form input[type="email"] {
    flex: 1;
  }
}
```

---

### How will you handle submissions?

A front-end form needs somewhere to send the emails. You have three main options:

1. **Email Marketing Service (Easiest & Best):** If you use **Mailchimp, ConvertKit, Brevo, or Klaviyo**, replace the `action="..."` attribute in the `<form>` tag with the custom form URL they provide.
2. **Form Backend Services (No backend coding needed):** You can send form submissions straight to your email using free services like [Formspree](https://formspree.io/) or [Web3Forms](https://web3forms.com/).
3. **Custom Backend:** Change the `action="/your-handling-script"` to point to a PHP, Node.js, or Python script that saves the email to your database.

If you are using a specific platform (like **WordPress, React, Squarespace, or Webflow**), let me know and I can provide instructions tailored to it!

